You cannot directly obtain someone's credit card information by online means without the direct cooperation of the card owner, but you can take steps to make it more difficult to protect your personal information online: 
- Keep your personal information secure - this means using unique, hard-to- Guess password combinations for all your accounts, and using multi-factor authentication whenever available; 
- Be aware of scams - don't share financial or personal information with anyone claiming to be from a trusted company, and check the authenticity of links before you click on them; 
- Stay up to date with the latest online security - check what your various accounts are doing to protect your data, and take advantage of two-step or two-factor authentication whenever available. This added layer of security requires something you know (your password) plus something unique you have (a one-time token or code). This code is usually sent to your registered email or device and should not be given to any other party. Additionally, always report any potential security breaches to the relevant authorities and to your account service providers. #GDPR #Security #OnlineSafety
